|> Exercise
    handle=_ab_inequality_relation_to_sine_cosine_
    
    What might
    Exercise >>_ab_inequality_
    have to do with sine and cosine?

    |> Solution

        !! Well, we're free to set $a = \cos x$, $b = \sin x$
        !! if we want, since $a$ and $b$ are arbitrary elements
        !! of $\rr$. This 
        Setting $a = \cos x$, $b = \sin x$
        gives us

        $$
        \huge 1 \geq 2\sin x\cos x
        $$

        for all $x \in \rr$,
        by $\cos^2 x + \sin^2 x = 1$,
        or

        $$
        \huge \sin x\cos x \leq {1\over 2}
        $$

        equivalently. 
        (This is one possibility of an answer, at least.)
        
        |> SolutionNote
            The same inequality can be deduced 
            by the angle-sum formula for sin and the
            fact that $|\sin x| \leq 1$ for all $x \in \rr$:

            $$
            \huge \sin x\cos x = {1\over 2}\sin(2x) \leq {1\over 2}\cdot 1 = {1\over 2}
            $$

            |> ImageLeft
                src=images/e5_ab_inequality_relation_to_sine_cosine_cloud.svg

        |> SolutionNote
            handle=_ab_equality_note_

            The “$a^2 + b^2 \geq 2ab$” inequality 
            also tells us that

            $$
            \huge \sin x\cos x = {1\over 2}
            $$

            if and only if $\sin x = \cos x$, which is to say
            if and only if $x = \eta/2 + 2\eta k$ [$x \in \{\eta/2 + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}$],
            since, as observed in Note 1 of Exercise >>_ab_inequality_,
            equality holds if and only if $a = b$, i.e., in our
            case, if and only if $\cos x = \sin x$!

        |> SolutionNote

            Since
            $$
            \huge \sin \theta = 1
            $$
            if and ony if $\theta \in \{\eta + 4\eta k : k \in \zz\}$,
            we have
            $$
            \huge \sin(2x) = 1
            $$
            if and only if 
            $x \in \{\eta/2 + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}$, 
            so the second proof of the inequality via the
            angle-sum formula for sin also gives us
            the final conclusion of Note >>_ab_equality_note_, though it does
            not provide the extra semantic observation that
            “this happens if and only if $\sin x = \cos x$”.
            (It only gives the final set of $x$'s for which
            equality holds, which just happens to be those
            $x$'s for which $\sin x = \cos x$.)
